  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/feee0332de88587b13bf58e4f860d76483646f41676c0bb335b4a9d5b32b3218.png)\n\nWe see that $p(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,-\\infty ),$ and since $[2,\\infty )$ lies completely inside $(-2,\\infty ),$ it is also continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=3,$ and so are not continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$",
